  "query_preview": "..\nTuesday night through Friday: The Inland Northwest is currently \nunder a moist south to southwest flow as a weak front gets hung up \nand dissipates west of the Cascades. A trailing shortwave will move \nthrough late tonight into early Wednesday morning, bringing light \nprecipitation to northeast Washington and north Idaho.  \n\nAn active synoptic pattern continues as a stronger frontal system \napproaches the Pacific Northwest Wednesday night into Thursday. \nDriven by a deeper southwesterly moisture plume and enhanced upper-\nlevel jet support, this system will penetrate farther inland. Early \nThursday morning, low-level southeasterly warm advection into the \neast slopes of the Cascades will be sufficient to overcome the \nCascade rain shadow, resulting in widespread precipitation across \nthe Inland Northwest. Snow levels rise to 5000 to 6000 feet \novernight, resulting in lowland rain and moderate to heavy high \nelevation snow. The primary valley exception is the upper Methow \nValley, where cold air damming may initially support wet snow or a \nrain/snow mix up toward Mazama. This is supported by models \nprojecting maximum wet bulb temperatures in the 0-4 km layer to be \nbelow freezing during the bulk of precipitation across the north \nCascades and the upper Methow Valley. National Blend of Models (NBM) \n10th to 90th percentile snowfall ranges from 6 to 12 inches at \nWashington Pass and up to 3 inches at Sherman Pass. Those planning \nto travel over Washington Pass should be prepared for difficult \ndriving conditions and winter driving conditions over Sherman Pass.\n\nA trailing cold front moves eastward late Thursday morning through \nthe afternoon, shifting precipitation into far eastern Washington \nand north Idaho while bringing an end to precipitation in central \nWashington. 25th to 75th percentile precipitation amounts indicates \n0.10 to 0.35 inches in the Columbia Basin and L-C Valley, 0.30 to \n0.80 inches across the Okanogan Valley, far eastern Washington, and \nthe north Idaho valleys, 0.40 to 1 inch in the Idaho Panhandle \nmountains, and 1.50 to 2.50 inches in the Cascades. Winds will also \nincrease with this front with southwest wind gusts up to 35 mph \nacross the Columbia Basin, Palouse, and Spokane area. Showers \npersist along the Cascades behind the front, where weak instability \n(100 to 200 J/kg of SBCAPE) west of the Cascade.\n\nFriday through Monday: There remains a significant amount of \nuncertainty in the pattern for Friday with models showing a \nshortwave moving through. There is little agreement on the strength \nof this system and the resultant precipitation. Ensembles have \ntrended drier for Saturday with a transient ridge overhead. This \nwill be very brief though with active weather returning early next \nweek. /vmt",
